# Requirements — {{TITLE}}

**As stated:** the requirement in the user's own words, one or two lines, unedited. If it was
reworded to be workable, the original goes here and the rewording goes under Assumptions.

## Acceptance criteria

Each one binary: true or false when you look at the result. "Fast" is not a criterion; "P95
under 400 ms on the existing benchmark" is.

| # | Criterion | Kind | How it is checked |
|---|---|---|---|
| AC1 | | happy / negative / non-functional | the test, measurement or observation that settles it |

At least one criterion should be **negative-path**. A criteria list with no negative rows has
not been thought through — it describes a demo, not a feature.

## Assumptions

Only ones not settled by the requirement itself.

| # | Assumption | What breaks if it is wrong |
|---|---|---|
| A1 | | |

## Explicitly not doing

What a broader reading of the requirement would have included, and why it is out. This list
is as load-bearing as the criteria — it is the difference between a scope decision and an
oversight.

| Not doing | Why |
|---|---|

## Coverage pass

Run in a fresh context, given only the requirement and the criteria above. Every finding
must resolve into a criterion or a row in *Explicitly not doing* — none may be dropped.

| # | Question | Finding | Resolved as |
|---|---|---|---|
| C1 | Which failure mode has no criterion? | | AC_n / not doing / none found |
| C2 | Which non-functional requirement is implied and unstated? | | |
| C3 | Which existing part of the system does this touch, unmentioned? | | |
| C4 | What does the phrasing assume is already true? | | |

## Open questions

Only the ones where two readings would produce materially different systems. Everything else
is an assumption above, not a question.

- [ ] question — and what each answer would change
